=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The CS department helpdesk complaint — "internet keeps blinking, everything freezes for a few seconds at random" — was investigated exhaustively across every layer of the network path from cs_hod through switch1, router0 (the 192.168.2.1 gateway), the RIP-routed WAN links (router0.eth2 → router1 → router2), and out to the internet segment.

**Affected component:** None confirmed. All subsystems are clean.

**Mechanism:** No fault mechanism was found. A genuine intermittent outage requires one of: a flap script cycling an interface down/up, an iptables/nftables drop rule, a tc qdisc rate-limit, a routing reconvergence cycle, or resource pressure on a forwarding device. None of these are present.

**Localization:** Router0.eth1 (the CS gateway port, 192.168.2.1) is UP/LOWER_UP with carrier=0 and zero errors. journalctl on router0 returned no link or interface events. dhcp_link_history on router0 and router1 both show network_down=0. pgrep found no flap/blink/bounce scripts. cron.d on all routers contains only standard system jobs.

**Root cause:** The intermittent connectivity symptom cannot be confirmed. No fault mechanism, no historical trace, and no active anomaly exist anywhere in the CS-to-internet path. The ticket report is unsubstantiated by live evidence.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user can reproduce the issue, capturing a ping flood to 192.168.2.1 and `ip -s link show eth1` on router0 during the freeze would provide the trace needed to identify any future transient.
=== END ===